{"hash":"3e336fe31f0156a3","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":83.597,"said":"Stop here, and look to your left, then to your right. You are standing in the third corridor, and it seems to go on for ever: a flagstone walkway between raised platforms, lined with massive pillars, some twelve hundred of them, under a ceiling painted with great medallions. Its four sides together run to about three thousand eight hundred feet, well over a kilometre, and it is counted the longest temple corridor in India. It was the gift of one Sethupathi, Muthuramalinga, who ruled from 1763 until his death in 1795. He did not live to be fifty, yet what he built has outlasted every king of his line. At the far western end of this corridor, stone figures of the king and his two ministers still stand, looking out over their work. And near them, where the corridor meets the path from the western gate, the floor and pillars form a pattern like a chessboard, so people call it the Chokkattan Mandapam, the chessboard hall. Pilgrims walk these flagstones dripping wet, because of what waits inside. Pass under the gopuram and turn left, into the open court. The wells are calling.\n\nDid you know? The pillars stand on platforms about five feet high, and many carry a painted yali, a mythical lion, leaning out over the walkway.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1087},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1103,"end":1233}],"words":[[0.098,0.409,0,4],[0.519,0.361,5,9],[1.12,0.18,11,14],[1.312,0.192,15,19],[1.516,0.12,20,22],[1.649,0.156,23,27],[1.817,0.397,28,32],[2.406,0.312,34,38],[2.73,0.12,39,41],[2.862,0.204,42,46],[3.079,0.397,47,52],[4.375,0.18,54,57],[4.567,0.132,58,61],[4.712,0.481,62,70],[5.204,0.108,71,73],[5.324,0.072,74,77],[5.409,0.3,78,83],[5.721,0.637,84,92],[6.574,0.132,94,97],[6.719,0.108,98,100],[6.839,0.373,101,106],[7.224,0.132,107,109],[7.368,0.216,110,112],[7.596,0.168,113,115],[7.776,0.228,116,119],[8.017,0.433,120,124],[8.63,0.036,126,127],[8.678,0.673,128,1